    {
      "id": "LR015",
      "section": "Case Statements",
      "start_line": 2189,
      "end_line": 2192,
      "text": "  - a `default:` statement is **always** included in order to avoid accidental\n  inference of latches, even if all cases are covered. In simulation, a case\n  expression that evaluates to `X` will not match any case and will behave as a\n  latch, leading to different behavior than synthesis if no default is specified.",
      "sha256": "671cab8965910cd22aa4aa60fb141c20ee77102e6da34dd8c28e743cf5ec15e0",
      "characters": 317,
      "use": "General synthesizable language/style guidance, not task requirements or a solution"
    },
    {
      "id": "LR013",
      "section": "Case Statements",
      "start_line": 2163,
      "end_line": 2164,
      "text": "Never use either the `full_case` or `parallel_case` pragmas. These pragmas can\neasily cause simulation/synthesis mismatches.",
      "sha256": "0a62f1572faa14cbaa96e8e4533cafb19af0b63d201516b0cdcb13111eb5c942",
      "characters": 124,
      "use": "General synthesizable language/style guidance, not task requirements or a solution"
    },
    {
      "id": "LR014",
      "section": "Case Statements",
      "start_line": 2181,
      "end_line": 2185,
      "text": "The `unique` prefix is recommended before all case statements, as it creates\nsimulation assertions that can catch certain mistakes. In some cases, `priority`\nmay be used instead of `unique`, though in such cases, cascaded ternary\nstructures should be the preferred way of representing priority encoders as\nthey are a more readable representation for priority encoders.",
      "sha256": "a7a74ae7a66911d61bb1c149b3937aaf2f2078f0497c6710b8b5b885ddddd562",
      "characters": 368,
      "use": "General synthesizable language/style guidance, not task requirements or a solution"
    }
  ],
